# Every text file is checked out with LF line ends, also on Windows (Git for Windows defaults to core.autocrlf=true):
# scripts/overleaf-login.sh is run with bash (Git Bash), which fails on CRLF, and the tests compare files byte for
# byte. Git's own detection keeps binary files as they are; the rules below say so explicitly for the common types.
* text=auto eol=lf
*.sh text eol=lf

*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.webp binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
*.zip binary
*.gz binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
*.pfb binary
*.bcmap binary
*.mp4 binary
*.webm binary
*.mov binary

# Third-party files are stored byte for byte (no line-ending conversion). This rule comes last so it wins over the
# ones above: public/vendor/vditor/dist/js/i18n/sv_SE.js, for one, has CRLF line ends upstream.
public/vendor/** -text
